Nate wrote:
> I have an eleventh level rogue in what has been a pretty lucky game so
> far. I found Frostbrand lying around on lvl 3 of the dungeon, and that
> made the early game much easier. I¢ve completed Sokoban (BoH) and
> gotten the luckstone from Mine¢s End (another break was that most of
> the deep mines were lighted levels).  The priest in Minetown is 
> coaligned and I¢ve been able to purchase some intrinsic protection.
>
> However, I¢ve not been able to find any source of reflection or magic
> Resistance, or any sources of wishes, and I¢m nervous about venturing
> much further into the Dungeons of Doom (I¢ve been as far down as lvl
> 10).   How do you usually approach games in which magic resistance and 
> reflection aren¢t found in the early game?

Reflection is only important in the early game, saving you from random
deaths to fire/cold/lightning wands before you have the appropriate
resistance, and against black dragons. Pretty much all other reflectable
attacks can be handled either with intrinsic resistances or via bagging
items (the only one that can't is yellow dragon breath, which is mostly
only an inconvenience to lategame characters), and dragons are
vulnerable to scrolls of genocide.

As such, I'd say that reflection is important to avoid early-game
deaths, but if you don't get it in the early game, it's not really worth
going for it at all. I've ascended without it at least once, probably
twice (I lose track, because I don't consider it that important an
extrinsic to aim for).

On the other hand, magic resistance will protect you from many of the
same attacks you'd want reflection to protect you from (it doesn't work
on elemental rays or black dragon breath, but both work on missile
attacks, striking, and wand of death attacks), and it stays useful all
game, being pretty much mandatory by the point that monsters can cast
touch of death (which includes the Castle if you're overlevelled or
unlucky). It's slightly less important in the early game, but still
useful for handling things like armor-destroying polymorph traps (and
later, armor-destroying monsters).

(That said, SDSM is considered a better wish than GDSM, early
game because reflection is more useful early game, late game
because there's less competition for equipment slots that can supply
magic resistance, than there is for equipment slots that can supply
reflection.)

